{"reviewer_participant_id":"actor:dependence-free-batch-h2-reviewer-fable-38","reviews":[{"case_id":"case:c80463fdc728955797e6","verdict":"demonstrated_issue","issue_class":"issue-class:repeated-authorized-independent-unit-entry-into-row-independent-procedure","bounded_statement":"For case:c80463fdc728955797e6 only, and based solely on the supplied task.md, data-description.md, data/input.csv, workflow/analysis.py, and results/report.md: the selected result (Welch two-sample t-test, t = 5.382, df = 44.17, p < 0.0001 on 48 leaf measurements) enters all four leaves from each of the 12 shrubs as separate independent observations, even though the data description designates shrub_id as the independent-unit column. Only 12 independent units exist (6 per cultivar), so the row-independent t-test's degrees of freedom (44.17) and its p-value are computed from pseudoreplicated rows, and the report's stated statistical conclusion is not supported at the strength claimed.","root_cause":"workflow/analysis.py pools tcrit_c values per cultivar directly from the 48 CSV rows (read_leaf_table appends one reading per row) and passes those leaf-level vectors to scipy.stats.ttest_ind, a procedure that assumes each row is an independent observation. shrub_id is read only to count distinct shrubs for the summary table; no aggregation to shrub means, mixed/hierarchical model, or cluster-adjusted variance is applied. Because four leaves share each shrub's genotype, soil, water status, and microclimate (per data-description.md), the same authorized independent unit is entered repeatedly into a row-independent procedure, inflating the effective sample size from 12 shrubs to 48 leaves and the Welch df to 44.17.","evidence_atoms":[{"description":"The data description declares the independent unit is the shrub, not the leaf, and states the clustering structure: 48 rows but only 12 shrubs, with leaves sharing a shrub_id sharing genotype, soil, water status, and microclimate.","observed_value":"Independent unit column: shrub_id; 48 rows from 12 shrubs (4 leaves per shrub)","source_spans":[{"path":"data-description.md","start_line":9,"end_line":11,"quoted_text":"rise steeply. Because four leaves come from each shrub, the file holds 48 rows\nbut only 12 shrubs; the four leaves sharing a shrub_id share that shrub's\ngenotype, soil, water status, and microclimate."},{"path":"data-description.md","start_line":22,"end_line":23,"quoted_text":"One row is: one leaf cut from one coffee shrub and put through a single temperature ramp\nIndependent unit column: shrub_id"}]},{"description":"The workflow appends one tcrit_c reading per CSV row into per-cultivar lists, so each shrub contributes four entries; shrub_id is captured only as a set of labels for counting, never used to aggregate or adjust.","observed_value":"readings[cultivar].append(...) executed once per row; shrub_id only added to a set","source_spans":[{"path":"workflow/analysis.py","start_line":27,"end_line":31,"quoted_text":"        for record in csv.DictReader(handle):\n            cultivar = record[GROUP_COLUMN]\n            readings[cultivar].append(float(record[TRAIT_COLUMN]))\n            shrubs[cultivar].add(record[\"shrub_id\"])\n    return readings, shrubs"}]},{"description":"The workflow runs a row-independent two-sample procedure (scipy.stats.ttest_ind with equal_var=False) directly on the 24-leaf-per-cultivar vectors, with Welch-Satterthwaite df computed from the leaf-level sample sizes.","observed_value":"stats.ttest_ind(first, second, equal_var=False) on n=24 leaves per group","source_spans":[{"path":"workflow/analysis.py","start_line":52,"end_line":55,"quoted_text":"    outcome = stats.ttest_ind(first, second, equal_var=False)\n    t_stat = float(outcome.statistic)\n    p_value = float(outcome.pvalue)\n    dof = float(welch_df(first, second))"}]},{"description":"The report explicitly states that every leaf measurement was entered as a single observation in the t-test.","observed_value":"each of 48 leaf rows treated as one independent observation","source_spans":[{"path":"results/report.md","start_line":12,"end_line":14,"quoted_text":"Every leaf measurement in the file was entered as a single observation, and the\ntwo cultivars were compared with Welch's two-sample t-test (unequal variances,\ntwo-sided) on `tcrit_c`."}]},{"description":"The selected result claims t = 5.382, df = 44.17, p < 0.0001 with n = 24 leaves per cultivar, and the report itself confirms the df derives from spending one degree of freedom per leaf row rather than per shrub.","observed_value":"t = 5.382, df = 44.17, p < 0.0001 computed from 48 rows representing only 12 independent shrubs","source_spans":[{"path":"results/report.md","start_line":25,"end_line":25,"quoted_text":"[selected-result] Welch two-sample t-test on 48 leaf measurements: t = 5.382, df = 44.17, two-sided p < 0.0001; mean tcrit_c is 1.500 C higher in Catuai (48.000 C, n = 24) than in Obata (46.500 C, n = 24)."},{"path":"results/report.md","start_line":27,"end_line":28,"quoted_text":"The test statistic above spends one degree of freedom per leaf measurement, so\nthe reported df of 44.17 comes from the 48 rows in the file."}]},{"description":"The CSV confirms repeated entries per independent unit: for example, shrub S01 contributes four leaf rows (L001-L004), all entered into the Catuai sample.","observed_value":"4 rows sharing shrub_id S01 with tcrit_c 49.5, 49.3, 49.1, 48.9","source_spans":[{"path":"data/input.csv","start_line":2,"end_line":5,"quoted_text":"L001,S01,Catuai,north_slope,sun_upper,49.5\nL002,S01,Catuai,north_slope,sun_mid,49.3\nL003,S01,Catuai,north_slope,shade_mid,49.1\nL004,S01,Catuai,north_slope,shade_lower,48.9"}]}],"counterevidence_atoms":[{"description":"The report transparently discloses the clustering by tabulating '6' shrubs sampled per cultivar alongside the 24 leaf measurements, so the shrub structure is not concealed; however, this disclosure does not change the test, which still uses all 48 rows as independent observations.","observed_value":"summary table reports 24 leaf measurements from 6 shrubs per cultivar","source_spans":[{"path":"results/report.md","start_line":18,"end_line":21,"quoted_text":"| cultivar | leaf measurements | shrubs sampled | mean tcrit_c (C) | SD (C) |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Catuai | 24 | 6 | 48.000 | 0.862 |\n| Obata | 24 | 6 | 46.500 | 1.059 |"}]}],"falsification_attempt":"I attempted to falsify the issue in three ways. (1) I searched workflow/analysis.py for any mechanism that would restore validity despite leaf-level entry — aggregation to shrub means, a mixed/hierarchical model, cluster-robust standard errors, or permutation at the shrub level; none exists, as shrub_id is only collected into a set for counting (lines 27-31) and the test is a plain scipy.stats.ttest_ind on leaf vectors (line 52). (2) I checked whether the data might actually be independent at the row level despite the description; data-description.md lines 9-11 and 22-23 explicitly state the opposite — four leaves per shrub share genotype, soil, water status, and microclimate, and the independent-unit column is shrub_id. Within-shrub tcrit_c values in the CSV are visibly clustered (e.g., S01: 49.5-48.9 versus S12: 45.4-44.8), consistent with strong within-shrub correlation. (3) I checked whether the reported statistics could plausibly have come from a shrub-level analysis: a correct shrub-level comparison would use n = 6 per cultivar with at most 10 degrees of freedom, whereas the report states df = 44.17 and itself attributes that df to the 48 rows (report lines 27-28). Computing shrub means from the CSV (Catuai: ~49.2, 48.7, 48.2, 47.8, 47.3, 46.8; Obata: ~48.0, 47.3, 46.9, 46.1, 45.7, 45.0) shows overlapping distributions, so the leaf-level pseudoreplication materially inflates the claimed statistical strength. All falsification attempts failed; the issue stands.","cross_case_evidence_used":false,"unresolved_material_questions":[],"self_reported_confidence":"high"}]}